  "description": "Tests the hypothesis hecke-tail-assembly-fit declared untested: blockPass's hpark collision condition, which is a property of the OTHER term. The tail applies the block twice, once per surviving term, so each application has the other as bystander. Measured: hpark HOLDS at the first application and FAILS at the second -- and it fails only because the block's LAST step emits the bystander, which is the cancellation that ends the chain. The bystander is never a word the block REWRITES, and SingleStepReduction reads its coefficient off the source side only. So hpark is too strong, not wrong: blockTransportRight / blockPassFromTwo / blockPass need the source-only relaxation that farComm_transport_right_sources already carries one level down.",
  "parameters": {
    "hpark": "for all r in the other terms, for all z, r != A ++ z",
    "block1_bystander": "hpark holds; not a source",
    "block2_bystander": "hpark FAILS; never a source; emitted by the block's LAST step and by no earlier one"
  },
